speechtext:
Mr. Speaker, I apologize to the Hon. Member for the interruption. We usually deal with this kind of matter at three o'clock on Thursday, but because of ongoing negotiations today we had to delay for a little while. But I think it

June 23, 1983
Business of the House
important that we put on the record to try and be as clear as possible the order of business for next week.
First, I would like to deal with an order that we have agreed upon related to the motion standing on the Order Paper in the name of the Minister of Justice (Mr. MacGuigan) relating to some amendments of the Constitution concerning aboriginal rights. Therefore I move, seconded by the Hon. Member for Simcoe North (Mr. Lewis):
That the Government Notice of Motion standing in the name of the Minister of Justice be now transferred for consideration under Government Orders at the next sitting of the House;
That the said motion shall be the first item to be considered under Government Orders on Monday, June 27, 1983;
That at 1 p.m. on Monday, June 27, 1983 the debate shall be suspended and the motion shall be deemed to have been referred to the Standing Committee on Indian Affairs and Northern Development;
That at 4 p.m. on Wednesday, June 29, 1983, the committee shall be deemed to have reported the motion and consideration of the motion shall be resumed; and
That no later than 5.45 p.m. on Wednesday, June 29, 1983, the Speaker shall interrupt any proceedings then before the House and shall put forthwith and successively any question or questions necessary for the disposal of the said motion.
